# DNA100C vendor HID - SETTINGS WRITE GROUP
# Captured 2026-10-31 via USBPcap, driven from the CLI (all devices on
# \.\USBPcap1, device address 12).
#
# HOW IT WAS PRODUCED
# Profile 1 power changed 36W -> 37W in EScribe, then "Upload Settings to
# Device". That one-watt change wrote EXACTLY ONE group. A full theme
# upload in the same session wrote ~91 of the same groups (568 large
# frames, 31,059 payload bytes), so the group is the atomic write unit and
# not an artifact of a small change.
#
# An upload with NOTHING changed writes nothing at all: it produces traffic
# byte-identical to a plain connect. EScribe diffs before it writes.
#
# Frame: 01 DE <inner_len> <op hi> <op lo> <payload>
#   f82a = host -> device (request)   f82b = device -> host (reply)
#
# WRITES RIDE THE SAME f82a OPCODE AS READS. No separate write opcode, no
# bulk endpoint, no CDC traffic. The machine has a single USB root hub, so
# one USBPcap filter covers every interface - this is not a coverage gap.
#
# GROUP SHAPE - the six write frames go out back-to-back with NO reply
# between them, bracketed by a prologue exchange and a terminator:
#
#   02 2f 1a 00 ..        prologue request  -> reply 03 62 1a 00 ..
#   35 2e 1e 00 ..        write 1
#   36 5a 1e 58 01 ..     write 2
#   36 5a 1e 70 02 ..     write 3
#   36 5a 1e 48 04 ..     write 4
#   36 5a 1e 60 05 ..     write 5
#   2d 3a 1e 78 06 ..     write 6
#   03 03 18 00 06 23 54  terminator
#   03 62 18 00 08 68 71  the single ACK for the whole group
#
# Byte 2 is 0x1e on every write frame. Byte 4 runs 01,02,04,05,06 - index
# 03 is absent in every group observed today.
#
# NOT DECODED, recorded so it is not mistaken for settled:
#  - whether byte 3 (00,58,70,48,60,78) is an address or a sequence tag
#  - declared inner_len is 0x3c (60) but only 58 payload bytes are present;
#    5 header + 60 exceeds the 63-byte HID report, so the declared length
#    and the carried length disagree and the reason is unknown
#  - what the prologue and terminator mean
#
# 0x7f runs are erased flash: 0xFF under the 7-bit encoding. That is the
# first confirmation of the encoding rule from the host->device direction.
#
# USB descriptors and control frames are EXCLUDED - string descriptors in a
# raw capture carry the device serial (kb/12). Raw captures stay out of the
# repo. Verified: 0 occurrences of the serial here, ASCII or UTF-16.

f82a  host->dev  len=0x08  02 2f 1a 00 4b 07 00 00
f82b  dev->host  len=0x09  03 62 1a 00 02 45 47 00 00
f82a  host->dev  len=0x3b  35 2e 1e 00 00 01 00 00 00 2b 60 0a 00 11 08 40 20 03 60 00 00 00 00 20 00 04 00 02 00 00 00 00 08 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 45 76 00
f82a  host->dev  len=0x3c  36 5a 1e 58 01 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 02 40 01 72 74 22 51 6c 35 2c 13 02 70 04 00 0f 76 00 40 10 00 64 0c 02 5c 0c 00 00 04 22
